Demonstrative_Examples.Demonstrative_Example[0].Body_Text[0]: The handler function uses global state (globalVar and logMessage), and it can be called by both the SIGHUP and SIGTERM signals. An attack scenario might follow these lines:
Demonstrative_Examples.Demonstrative_Example[0].Body_Text[1].xhtml:ul.xhtml:li[0].xhtml:div: The program begins execution, initializes logMessage, and registers the signal handlers for SIGHUP and SIGTERM.
Demonstrative_Examples.Demonstrative_Example[0].Body_Text[1].xhtml:ul.xhtml:li[1].xhtml:div: The program begins its "normal" functionality, which is simplified as sleep(), but could be any functionality that consumes some time.
Demonstrative_Examples.Demonstrative_Example[0].Body_Text[1].xhtml:ul.xhtml:li[2].xhtml:div: The attacker sends SIGHUP, which invokes handler (call this "SIGHUP-handler").
Demonstrative_Examples.Demonstrative_Example[0].Body_Text[1].xhtml:ul.xhtml:li[3].xhtml:div: SIGHUP-handler begins to execute, calling syslog().
Demonstrative_Examples.Demonstrative_Example[0].Body_Text[1].xhtml:ul.xhtml:li[4].xhtml:div: syslog() calls malloc(), which is non-reentrant. malloc() begins to modify metadata to manage the heap.
Demonstrative_Examples.Demonstrative_Example[0].Body_Text[1].xhtml:ul.xhtml:li[5].xhtml:div: The attacker then sends SIGTERM.
